findings.body:
The session-wide autouse fixture now calls `prefect_test_harness(server_timeout=60)`. The function's signature is `prefect_test_harness(server_startup_timeout: int | None = 30)`, so this raises `TypeError: unexpected keyword argument 'server_timeout'` on the first test. Because the fixture is `autouse=True` and session-scoped, every test in `prefect-gcp` will error, so CI is red regardless of the `_resolve_path` change.

::code{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/tests/conftest.py" lines="14-20"}

::suggestion{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/tests/conftest.py" lines="19-19"}
    with prefect_test_harness(server_startup_timeout=60):

The PR description says the goal is a 60s timeout, which matches `server_startup_timeout`. Also confirm the 60s value actually fixes the Python 3.11+ flake before keeping the comment that cites #16397.

The new guard uses `self.bucket_folder in path`, which tests containment, not a prefix. Any key that contains the folder name anywhere skips the prefix and lands at the bucket root or in the wrong place. I ran the same logic standalone with `bucket_folder="results/"`:

- `"myresults/abc"` → `"myresults/abc"` (should be `"results/myresults/abc"`)
- `"data/results/x.parquet"` → `"data/results/x.parquet"` (should be `"results/data/results/x.parquet"`)

The object is silently written outside the configured folder. That breaks folder-scoped IAM, lifecycle rules, and `list_folder` assumptions. Reads go through the same function, so a round-trip still "works", which is why this is easy to miss. Folder names like `results/`, `output/`, or `data/` make this likely in real use.

::diff{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/prefect_gcp/cloud_storage.py" lines="726-737"}

The sibling helper `_join_bucket_folder` already uses a prefix check (`startswith`), and the PR description says this fix mirrors that one. The guard should match:

::suggestion{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/prefect_gcp/cloud_storage.py" lines="734-735"}
        if self.bucket_folder and path.startswith(self.bucket_folder):
            return path

Add a negative test for `myresults/abc` with folder `results/` so the substring case cannot regress.
